Assignment title : Visual Promt Studio 1.2

Prompt: Create a Synthwave/Retro 1980s album cover with a futuristic city at night, a glowing neon sunset, a geometric grid, laser lines, and a futuristic chrome sports car as the main subject. Use bright neon pink, purple, blue, and orange against a dark sky, with strong value contrast and the sunset as the brightest area. Create depth by making the grid larger in the foreground and smaller toward the horizon, with the car overlapping the grid and the distant buildings becoming smaller and less detailed. Show motion through the laser lines, moving car, and setting sun, while diagonal lines guide the viewer toward the city. Create unity with repeated neon colors, geometric shapes, and glowing textures, but add variety with soft clouds of smoke around the sun. Use an asymmetrical balance by placing the large car on one side and balancing it with the neon sun and city skyline on the other. 

Vocabulary Breakdown:
Value: The value allows something to appear either light or dark. For my design i used dark colors in the background and bright neon colors in the lights and sunset to contrast and make areas stand out more. 
Space: is the area around, inside, and between objects. In this design I create depth by making the foreground large at the beginning where you start to see it and smaller towards the horizon. Also, the overlapping of the car on the grid and buildings towards the back makes it look three dimensional. 
Unity: Unity is what helps make all the different parts of the artwork feel like they go together. Here I use unity by repeating the neon colors, the lines, and the geometric shapes.  
Balance: Balance is the distribution of weight throughout an artwork. I use an asymmetrical balance by placing the large dark car on one side and on the other a bright big sun with a skyline going between the two.  
Rhythm: Rhythm is when an element repeats in a way that leads the viewers' eyes thought the whole artwork. The repetition of grid lines and the neon lights create rhythm and guide the viewer towards the horizon. 
